  Full history of every cause, live and retired:
    A ✔ FIXED — `[Logic::Zero; N]` array locals (bit-width inference)
    B ✔ FIXED — fully-qualified type paths (`copper_core::Clock<MainClk>`)
    C ✔/☐ SPLIT — `while` was TWO features, and the two modules filed here needed
        different ones. Third cause in this file grouped by error MESSAGE rather
        than mechanism (after D and J); worth reading as a pattern.
        C-a ✔ FIXED — `while <cond> { … tick; }` is a data-dependent WAIT, and is
            exactly `loop { if !<cond> { break; } … }`: test first, tick last,
            the supported ordering. Desugared before control extraction
            (`control_extract::desugar_tick_waits`), so it lands on machinery
            already verified end to end rather than adding control flow.
            UNBLOCKED det_010_awaits — which was NOT the module filed here, and
            was recorded under "tick inside a conditional branch". Its `while`
            made `contains_unflattenable_control` decline the whole body, so the
            tick-in-branch fell through to the linear path and reported the error
            there. Another first-error-masking-the-second.
        C-b ☐ a `while` with NO tick is combinational: it must fully unroll, which
            needs a trip count known at compile time. `while` only implies that;
            `for` states it. Left refused, but the diagnostic now names `for`
            instead of advising "add a tick", which is the wrong fix for a
            combinational module. bsg_gray_to_binary was the only instance and is
            now written as a `for` — its own test fixture already used that
            spelling, so the `while` form had never been transpiled.
        STILL BLOCKED, on its own causes: uart/rx — now cause M (a tick inside a
        counted `for`), re-measured 2026-08-25. K WAS recorded here as its first
        blocker; K is fixed and uart/rx did not move, which is consistent with
        K's own entry. Behind M sit `continue` in a nested loop (pinned as
        refused), `|=`, and a bare integer local.
    D-a ✔ FIXED — file-scope `const` items now lower to `localparam`s.
        Unblocked bsg_encode_one_hot AND bsg_adder_one_hot. See the write-up below.
    D-b ✔ FIXED — array-typed ports lower as PACKED 2-D. Unblocked mux and
        bsg_mux_one_hot. Scoped first in design_docs/ARRAY_PORT_ABI.md (three
        options, measured); see the write-up below.
    E ✔ FIXED — `Memory` now transpiles ......... dual_port_ram. Both CPUs stay
        blocked, but on their OWN causes (re-measured): rv32i_cpu on G,
        rv32i_cpu_pipelined on F — not on memory.
    F ☐ `Vec` ports ....................... rv32i_cpu_pipelined, rv32i_cpu
        (rv32i_cpu joined this cause once the G panic stopped masking it)
    K ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24) — **A LOOP WHOSE BODY ENDS IN ANOTHER TICK-BEARING
        LOOP** now flattens. It does NOT by itself unblock uart_tx / uart_rx /
        uart/rx: those need the counted `for` as well (see below — the ordering
        recorded here was backwards, and why is worth reading).
        `control_extract::tick_is_last_statement` required a DIRECT
        `clk.tick().await` as the last statement of a nested loop's body, so a
        loop whose clock boundary came from a loop INSIDE it was declined and the
        module fell through to the linear path. It is now
        `body_ends_at_a_clock_boundary`, which admits a second spelling: the
        body's last statement may be another tick-bearing loop.
        THE FLATTENER ALREADY HANDLED THE SHAPE. Measured before changing
        anything: `lower_into`'s nested-loop path takes the outer back edge from
        the inner loop's `break` continuation, which is exactly what the old note
        below said "the fix has to establish". Only the gate refused it. The two
        real pieces of work were the two things the gate had been masking:
        1. `find_tick_in_expr` DID NOT DESCEND INTO A NESTED `loop`. Control
           extraction ends by reusing a real tick node from the source; that
           search and the gate must agree about where a tick can live, and they
           had drifted. It went unnoticed while every flattenable module also had
           a tick at the top level of its own loop. Cause K is the first shape
           whose ONLY tick is inside a nested loop: the pass declined silently and
           the linear path blamed an unrelated construct. Same
           two-halves-disagree bug class as the panic that G fixed.
        2. REACHABILITY ACCEPTED A PROGRAM THE SIMULATOR LIVELOCKS ON — the
           serious one, filed as its own entry under SEMANTICS below.
        ALSO FIXED: THE DIAGNOSTIC. `chir_lower::nested_loop_error` now sorts the
        three reasons a nested loop lands there (no tick at all / a tick in
        statement position but not last / ticks only inside branches) instead of
        printing the tick-ORDERING rule for all of them — unfollowable advice for
        two of the three. It also descends to the innermost loop at fault, so a
        well-formed outer loop is not named for an inner loop's problem.
        THE ORDERING IN THE OLD NOTE WAS BACKWARDS, and the reason is structural,
        not incidental: it said to verify against "a hand-written nested-wait DUT
        (no `for` involved)" before counted delays. NO SUCH WELL-FORMED DUT
        EXISTS. A nested loop's tick must be LAST, so any inner loop that can exit
        tests before it ticks and can therefore exit having ticked zero times; an
        outer loop whose only boundary is one of those is a zero-time cycle. So
        with `loop` alone, cause K's BACK EDGE is not writable at all. The counted
        `for` is what breaks the deadlock — `for _ in 0..N { tick }` always ticks
        AND terminates without an exit test — which makes counted repetition the
        prerequisite for K's remaining half, not the other way round.
        WHAT IS PINNED NOW: `tests/nested_boundary_equivalence.rs` (sim ≡
        Verilator on the ENTRY path — entering the outer loop costs no cycle) and
        `copper-codegen/tests/unsupported_constructs.rs` (the shape transpiles;
        the livelock shape is refused; the branch-only shape gets its own
        message). The back edge is checkable once `for` lands.
        The UART shape that still needs `for`:
            for i in 0..8 { tx_serial.write(byte[i]); for _ in 0..CLKS { tick } }
    M ✔ FIXED (2026-08-25) — **A TICK INSIDE A COUNTED `for`** is counted
        REPETITION, and now desugars to a counter-driven `loop`.
        `control_extract::desugar_counted_loops` rewrites

            for <var> in <start>..<end> { <B>; clk.tick().await; }
          ⟶ let mut <var>: u32 = <start>;
            loop {
                if <var> >= <end> { break; }      // empty range + the loop's test
                <B>
                <var> = <var> + 1;
                if <var> >= <end> { break; }      // leave BEFORE the last tick
                clk.tick().await;
            }
            clk.tick().await;                     // …which is this one

        A desugar rather than a new state shape, for the same reason C-a was: the
        result lands on `lower_into`'s nested-loop path, already verified end to
        end. `<var>` becomes a register and the loop is ONE state — a UART bit
        period is 434 cycles, so unrolling was never an option.
        Both shapes cause M was filed for are covered, which is why it is
        REPETITION and not delay:
            for _ in 0..CLKS_PER_BIT { clk.tick().await; }              // timing
            for i in 0..8 { tx.write(byte[i]); for _ in 0..CLKS { tick } }  // data
        The second nests, and `i` is a counter REGISTER, so `byte[i]` lowers to a
        dynamic bit select (`CHIRExpr::DynBit`) rather than an unrolled slice.

        **WHY THE LAST TICK IS HOISTED OUT OF THE LOOP — the part that is not
        obvious, and the reason the first attempt was reverted.** The naive shape
        (one test at the top, tick last, nothing after the loop) transpiles and is
        WRONG BY A CYCLE. Measured on `for _ in 0..3 { tick }`: the simulator
        rewrote the output every 3rd cycle, the transpiled module every 4th.
        Breaking out of a nested loop is not a clock boundary — `lower_into`
        inlines the enclosing loop's continuation into the breaking path and
        carries on in the same cycle. But when that continuation contains no
        boundary of its own, the lowering falls off the end and emits
        `pc = <enclosing head>`, and the FSM spends a cycle on the transition.
        With the tick left inside the loop, a counted delay's continuation IS
        empty, so every pass cost one cycle more than the source asks for.
        THAT IS CAUSE K'S BACK EDGE. K's own entry records it as unverified —
        "the back edge is checkable once `for` lands" — because no well-formed
        hand-written program could reach it. This is the first thing that could,
        and it was broken. Hoisting the final tick into the ENCLOSING statement
        list means a break always continues into a real clock boundary, so the
        shape is never produced: the divergent form is made unwritable rather
        than the flattener taught to special-case it, the same disposition as the
        tick-ordering rule. The second exit test is what pays for it — the loop
        leaves before the final tick and the hoisted one supplies it — and `<B>`
        is not duplicated.
        THE ALTERNATIVE, REJECTED WITH ITS REASON: teach `lower_into` to INLINE
        the enclosing head's body at a zero-time back edge instead. That is the
        principled fix and would also unblock `continue` (cause O — the same
        problem: `continue` is a zero-time jump to the head). It needs the head's
        LOWERED body at a point where it is still being lowered, so it means a
        second pass that splices marked `pc = H` assignments after every state
        body exists — surgery on the most timing-sensitive pass in the codebase,
        for a shape the desugar can simply avoid producing. Left undone
        deliberately; cause O is where it would be paid for.

        `>=` RATHER THAN `==`: `for i in 5..3` yields nothing in Rust, and `i == 3`
        would spin forever. A statically empty range is dropped outright, since the
        hoisted tick would otherwise be a cycle the source does not have.
        ☐ KNOWN GAP, and it is the same one the analysis already has: when the
        bounds are not literals (`0..CLKS_PER_BIT`), emptiness is not decidable in
        the desugar, and an empty one would produce that spurious cycle.
        `copper_analysis::may_exit_without_tick` has the identical hole for the
        identical reason (it treats a `for` as a guaranteed boundary, which an
        empty range is not). Both need const evaluation to close, and they should
        be closed together.

        THE DIAGNOSTIC HALF, also fixed, and it was not about lowering at all.
        uart/rx used to report the well-formed repeating wait on line 55 for
        having its tick in the wrong place. Declining a module is deliberate — the
        linear path usually diagnoses better than the flattener could — but it only
        sees what it REACHES, so a construct that stopped the flattening elsewhere
        left it blaming whatever it met first. `control_extract::unflattenable_reason`
        now names that construct, `transpile_fir` prefers it over the downstream
        error, and uart/rx reports `continue` at 64:12 with its own span. A
        MALFORMED NESTED LOOP is deliberately NOT named this way —
        `chir_lower::nested_loop_error` splits that three ways and points at the
        innermost loop at fault, and duplicating that reasoning in the gate is
        exactly the two-halves-drift bug this file keeps recording.

        WHAT IT UNBLOCKED: nothing in examples/, as cause M's own entry predicted.
        uart/rx is behind `continue` (cause O) and uart_tx/uart_rx behind the
        file-level `spawn_uart` rejection (cause H). The anchors are therefore
        purpose-built DUTs, which is the right shape for this anyway:
          - tests/counted_delay_equivalence.rs — `for _ in 0..3 { tick }`, sim ≡
            Verilator, with a distinct byte per cycle so an off-by-one period
            shows as a different value rather than the same one later. THIS TEST
            FAILED on the naive desugar and is what measured the wasted cycle.
          - tests/counted_repetition_equivalence.rs — the nested serialiser shape,
            plus an INDEPENDENT property (the line must carry the byte LSB-first
            at two cycles per bit), so the FSM has to be right and not merely
            self-consistent.
          - copper-codegen/tests/unsupported_constructs.rs — the counter is a
            counter and not an unroll, the empty range costs nothing, a tick that
            is not last is refused with a `for`-specific message, and a module
            declined for one construct is not reported against another.

        A FRONT-END DISAGREEMENT IT SURFACED, worth recording as its own thing:
        `register_reconciliation` failed, because codegen now emits a register for
        `i` that `copper_analysis::infer_registers` did not name. The analysis was
        right to be asked and wrong in its answer — `DefinedInLoop` collected `let`
        bindings and assignment targets but not a `for`'s pattern binding, so
        `for i in 0..8 { …; tick; }` produced no register at all in the shared
        inference while the language's central rule says it must. Fixed in
        `copper-analysis` (gated on the body actually ticking; a tick-free `for`
        unrolls and its variable is an elaboration index, not state). The
        synthesized `__copper_ctr<N>` is genuinely nameless in the source — `_`
        binds nothing — so it joins `pc` on the test's synthetic list.
        THE GUARD EARNED ITS KEEP: it caught the disagreement the same day it was
        introduced, which is what it exists for.

    O ✔ FIXED (2026-08-25) — **`continue`** in the module's top-level loop.
        It is a back edge that costs NO clock cycle, and the extracted FSM has no
        way to say that: its shape is `loop { match pc { … }; clk.tick().await; }`,
        so every `pc` transition spends exactly one cycle. The only way to spend
        none is to be in the same state body — i.e. to INLINE the target. That is
        the rule `break` already follows (it inlines the enclosing loop's
        continuation rather than transitioning to it); cause O applies it to the
        back edge.
        `lower_into` cannot do the inlining itself — at the point it lowers a
        `continue`, the head's body is the thing being lowered further up its own
        call stack. So it emits `__copper_goto = H` and
        `control_extract::splice_zero_time_gotos` substitutes state H's statements
        once every body exists. That is the "second pass that splices state bodies
        into marked zero-time transitions" cause M's entry describes.
        ORDER MATTERS: the splice runs AFTER `hoist_cross_state_locals`. Splicing
        first would copy the target's `let`s into another state, and a temporary
        local to one state would then look cross-state and be promoted to a
        flip-flop nobody asked for.
        A GOTO CYCLE — a chain of zero-time transitions returning to its start —
        makes `extract_control` DECLINE the module rather than emit anything, and
        the marker name is checked to have vanished before the arms are built. A
        leaked `__copper_goto` would otherwise become an assignment to an
        undeclared name downstream, which is the quiet kind of failure this pass
        must not produce.

        **THE ANALYSIS HOLE THAT HAD TO BE CLOSED FIRST — the serious half.**
        `copper_analysis` modelled a top-level `continue` as a FALL-THROUGH to the
        next statement, which is the one thing it does not do. `loop_ctx` is empty
        in a top-level loop (a hardware loop never `break`s), so `continue` took
        the fallback target. `loop { if c { continue; } clk.tick().await; }` then
        appeared to reach the tick on every path and PASSED reachability, while the
        real program returns to the head having ticked zero times — a zero-time
        cycle the simulator livelocks on.
        Nothing exercised it because codegen refused `continue` outright; emitting
        one without fixing this would have shipped exactly the failure cause K
        recorded as "REACHABILITY ACCEPTED A PROGRAM THE SIMULATOR LIVELOCKS ON".
        `Builder::top_head` now routes it to the loop head, in BOTH front-ends
        (the macro re-runs the same check), and the two analysis tests pin both
        directions: the tickless `continue` is rejected, and one reached only
        after a counted `for` has ticked is not.
        RECORD THE CLASS: a fallback (`map_or(next, …)`) standing in for a case the
        code had no target for. It is not a wrong answer, it is a MISSING one
        wearing a default's clothes — and it read as deliberate for as long as
        nothing could reach it.

        TWO SMALLER THINGS IT NEEDED, both silent:
          - `expr_diverges` did not count `continue`. `if halt { continue; }`
            contains no tick and no `break`, so it was pushed through as an
            ordinary combinational statement and the `continue` inside it was never
            lowered at all. It belongs there for exactly the reason `break` does.
          - `loop_needs_extraction` did not fire on a `continue`, so an otherwise
            linear body (`loop { tick; if c { continue; } … tick; }`) fell through
            to the phase FSM, which cannot express a mid-body re-entry.

        NESTED `continue` IS STILL REFUSED, and the reason is the rotation, not
        effort: a nested loop's head state holds `C ; W` — the post-tick tail
        wrapped onto the pre-tick prefix — so re-entering it would run the
        statements the source places AFTER the tick this `continue` never reached.
        The correct target is the start of `W`, which is not a state at all; it is
        inlined into whatever precedes the loop. Note such a `continue` is also
        hard to write well-formed: the enclosing loop's own tick must be LAST, so
        the tick on the `continue`'s path has to come from a loop nested inside it.

        WHAT IT UNBLOCKED, measured: nothing in examples/ — 28/34 unchanged.
        uart/rx moved to a NEW blocker, and it is one already decided rather than
        one to build: the `for i in 0..8` at 69:8 delays first and samples
        `rx_serial` after, so its tick is not the body's last statement. That is a
        post-tick `In` read — the seam CLOSED AS REFUSED BY DESIGN on 2026-08-24
        (see THE MID-PHASE READ SEAM). The module is writable in the language, by
        aligning 1.5 bit periods and then sampling BEFORE each delay, but that is a
        rewrite of the example's design and not a transpiler change. Behind it sit
        `|=` and a bare integer local.
        NOTE THE CONSERVATISM, so it is not mistaken for the rule: the desugar
        refuses ANY `for` whose tick is not last, including one that only touches
        REGISTERS afterwards — which the seam measured as correct. It matches the
        existing nested-`loop` gate rather than being more permissive than it; if
        that gate is ever relaxed to key on the post-tick READ instead of the
        position, both should move together.

        CAUSE M'S HOISTED TICK COULD NOW BE REMOVED with this machinery — the
        wasted back edge M works around is a zero-time fall-through, and the
        splice handles exactly that. DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: M is verified green
        against Verilator, and rewriting its desugar to depend on the newer pass
        is churn with real risk for no behavioural gain. Worth doing only if a
        third caller needs it.

        → tests/continue_equivalence.rs (sim ≡ Verilator; the reference model is
          written from the SOURCE and encodes the zero-cycle back edge, so a
          `continue` lowered as `pc = 0` fails it — verified by reverting),
          copper-codegen/tests/unsupported_constructs.rs (transpiles, no marker
          leaks, the tickless form is still rejected, nested is still refused),
          copper-analysis/tests/analysis_semantics.rs (both reachability
          directions).

    ☐ MINOR, found in passing: Verilator rejects a port named `abort`
        (`SYMRSVDWORD` — it matches a C++ common word) and the equivalence harness
        runs `-Wall`, so the build fails. `vlir_lower`'s legalizer reserves
        SystemVerilog keywords but not C++ ones, so a legal Copper name can produce
        SystemVerilog that Verilator will not compile. Worked around in the cause-O
        test by renaming the port; the legalizer is the real fix.

    G ✔ FIXED — the transpiler no longer PANICS on any example. rv32i_cpu now
        reports a clean error, and it turns out to be cause F (a `Vec<Bits<32>>`
        port at rv32i_cpu.rs:190), not a memory or control-flow limit — the panic
        was masking the module's actual first blocker. See the fix write-up below.
    H ☐ no structural parent module .............. uart/system (uart_tx, uart_rx)
        spawn_uart is a plain sim-wiring fn, so the UART has no hierarchy module
        to transpile. two_domain_top shows the pattern already works.
    I ✔ FIXED — a branch-local `let` with a COMPUTED initializer read as a latch.
        Unblocked bsg_counter_up_down. See the write-up below.
    J ✔/☐ SPLIT — the two modules filed here share an error MESSAGE, not a cause.
        Measured directly rather than read off the message, which is what the
        earlier "bare integer local" framing got wrong:
        J-a ✔ FIXED — an integer local from a CONST EXPRESSION had no inferable
            width. `let mut k = WIDTH - 1;` (bsg_gray_to_binary) asked both sides
            of the subtraction for a type: `WIDTH` is not a signal so it was not
            in the symbol table, and `1` is a bare literal — both ambiguous, so
            the module was rejected. Parameters and file-scope consts are now
            seeded into the symbol table as 32-bit, which is what they emit as
            (`parameter int`/`localparam int`) and the width a `for`-loop
            variable already got. Pinned by transpile_inference_gaps.rs::
            an_integer_local_from_a_{const_expression,generic_const_param}_infers_a_width.
            THIS UNBLOCKED NOTHING BY ITSELF: bsg_gray_to_binary now reports its
            real remaining blocker, a `while` loop (cause C). Same
            first-error-was-masking-the-second pattern as rv32i_cpu and D-a.
        J-b ☐ ripple_carry_adder (27:8) is the TUPLE-RETURNING HELPER gap, not a
            width gap at all — `let (s, c) = full_adder(…)`. Already pinned by
            transpile_inference_gaps.rs::tuple_returning_helper_is_a_known_transpile_gap;
            it was only filed here because it prints the same message.
  NO LONGER BLOCKED (verified by the 2026-08-25 sweep, absent from the failing
  list): det_010_awaits, which C-a unblocked. It had been filed under C, then
  re-filed as "tick inside a conditional branch (65:8)" — and neither was its own
  cause. Its `while` made `contains_unflattenable_control` decline the whole body,
  so the tick-in-branch fell through to the linear path and was reported there.

  ── UART/RX, REWRITTEN TO SAMPLE BEFORE THE DELAY (2026-08-25) ───────────────
  ✔ 28/34 → 29/34, and the first coverage gain that came from the EXAMPLE rather
  than the transpiler. Worth reading for that reason: three causes in a row (M,
  `continue`, then this) each unblocked uart/rx and each revealed another blocker,
  and the last one was not a missing feature. Its data loop read `rx_serial`
  AFTER a bit-period delay — a post-tick `In` read, the seam CLOSED AS REFUSED BY
  DESIGN on 2026-08-24.
  THE REWRITE, and why it is not a behaviour change: pre-load ONE bit period after
  the start-bit check, then have each iteration SAMPLE FIRST and DELAY LAST. The
  instants sampled are identical (centre of data bit i, for i in 0..8), the eighth
  iteration's trailing delay lands on the centre of the stop bit where the
  separate stop-bit wait used to leave it, and all five self-check sections in the
  example's own `main()` pass with the same bytes. Two smaller changes came with
  it, each removing a recorded blocker:
    - a SHIFT REGISTER instead of `byte_val |= 1 << i` over a bare integer. The
      byte is assembled across eight clock boundaries, so it is a register, and a
      `[Logic; 8]` filled one element per iteration is assigned on some paths and
      not others — measured: "would infer a latch". Shifting right and inserting
      at the top is what a real receiver builds anyway. This is also the corpus's
      first register READ AFTER ITS OWN UPDATE in one segment
      (`byte_val = byte_val >> 1;` then `byte_val = byte_val | 0x80`), i.e. the
      first real exercise of cause N.
    - `CLKS_PER_HALF_BIT` as a named `const` rather than `CLKS_PER_BIT / 2` at the
      use site. `/` is a real divider in a HARDWARE expression and is refused
      there; a file-scope const is elaboration-time arithmetic and already lowers
      to `localparam int … = CLKS_PER_BIT / 2`. The two paths are right to differ.

  IT TRANSPILED AND WAS WRONG — the part worth keeping. Acceptance is not
  correctness (this file's own "LINT-CLEAN IS NOT THE SAME AS TRANSPILES", one
  step further along), and the module reached Verilator only because an
  equivalence test was written for it. `examples/uart/rx.rs` runs the SIMULATOR
  only; nothing had ever checked its SystemVerilog.

  ✔ FIXED — **`pc` WAS ALWAYS `u8`, AND NOTHING CHECKED THE STATE COUNT.**
     uart/rx flattens to 788 states, so `pc = 256` wrapped onto `0` and the emitted
     `case` had overlapping arms — well-formed SystemVerilog that runs the wrong
     state. Verilator's CASEOVERLAP caught it; with that lint off it would simply
     have executed the wrong arm. `pc_ty(states)` now picks `u8`/`u16`/`u32`, at
     the one point where the count is final. The type and the `match`-arm literal
     suffix are ONE decision (a mismatch is a WIDTHEXPAND on the case items), which
     is why they were a pair of constants and are now a pair of returns.
     RECORD THE CLASS: a fixed-width encoding with no bound check on what it
     encodes. It held for as long as nothing generated 256 states, and the first
     design that did was silently miscompiled rather than refused.

  ✔ FIXED — **A ZERO-TIME BACK EDGE STILL COST A CYCLE**, and this is the case
     cause M's entry recorded as deliberately not fixed ("cause M's hoisted tick
     could now be removed with this machinery"). It was not merely cosmetic.
     `lower_into` ends a body that ran off its end with `pc = <head>`, and that is
     right for a NESTED loop — the rotation removed the body's trailing tick, so
     the fall-through stands for it — but wrong for the module's own loop, whose
     body genuinely ends with statements after its last tick. Minimal repro:
         loop { for _ in 0..3 { tick } dv.write(One); tick; dv.write(Zero); }
     The simulator repeats every 4 cycles, the FSM every 5: `dv.write(Zero)`
     belongs to the next iteration's first cycle (an `Out` HOLDS until rewritten)
     and was given a state of its own. Note the LINEAR path REJECTS trailing
     statements after the last tick (`trailing_combinational_statements_in_a_
     multi_tick_loop_are_rejected`) while control extraction accepted them and
     mis-timed them — the two paths disagreeing again, and silently.
     FIX: `FallThrough` on `LoopCtx`. `ZeroTime` emits cause O's goto marker and
     the splice inlines the head; `AfterTick` keeps `pc = head`. It is a property
     of the CONTEXT, not the call site, which is what makes a `break` come out
     right: its continuation is lowered in the ENCLOSING context, and whether that
     continuation's end stands for a removed tick depends on which loop it belongs
     to rather than on where the `break` was written.
     So cause O's machinery paid for itself twice, and the deferral in cause M's
     entry was the wrong call — the shape was reachable, not hypothetical.

  ◐ D1 FALSE NEGATIVE, found here and NOT fixed. With the above corrected, the
     same repro with a plain `Out` still diverges by exactly one cycle, uniformly:
     the simulator observes a write issued after a tick in that tick's POST-EDGE
     SETTLE, while the FSM puts it in the state that runs the next cycle. That is
     the pre-tick alignment family and `RegOut` is immune by construction
     (measured: the `RegOut` spelling of the same repro passes). But
     `unprotected_pretick_out_write` did NOT flag it, because its condition
     requires the segment to also ASSIGN A REGISTER, and this segment assigns
     none — it only writes outputs. So the guardrail has a hole for exactly the
     shape a pulse generator has.
     uart/rx's `rx_dv`/`rx_byte` are `RegOut` now, which is independently the
     right call (a one-cycle pulse written before the tick that publishes it IS
     write-before-tick Moore). The guard's condition is the open question; see
     design_docs/PRETICK_ALIGNMENT_GUARDRAIL.md before widening it, since three
     candidate fixes are already recorded there as rejected with evidence.

  ☐ 788 STATES FOR A UART RECEIVER — correct now, but absurd, and the number does
     not depend on CLKS_PER_BIT (434 and 8 both give 788), so it is structural
     duplication and not unrolling. `lower_into` RE-LOWERS a nested loop's break
     continuation at every `break` site, and the desugared counted `for` has two
     of them, so each nesting level roughly doubles. The `None` branch already
     solves this for its own case by cloning the LOWERED body so the copies share
     the sub-states their ticks allocated; the break path should do the same.
     Not a correctness bug (sim ≡ Verilator passes), but 16k lines of SystemVerilog
     and a 788-way `case` is real area, and it is what makes `pc` need 16 bits.

  → tests/uart_rx_equivalence.rs — the receiver at an 8-cycle bit period (434 is
    trace, not coverage), decoding three frames. It is the densest module in the
    corpus: a data-dependent `while` wait, a `continue` that must cost no cycle,
    four counted `for` delays (one nested inside a `for` whose body does work), a
    shift register across eight clock boundaries, and a register read after its
    own update. Causes M, N and O all meet in it, which is why it found two bugs
    that none of their own purpose-built tests did.

  ── RE-MEASUREMENT OF EVERY REMAINING CAUSE, 2026-08-24 ─────────────────────
  Prompted by four causes in a row turning out to be masking (D, J, C, and
  det_010_awaits). Method: remove the REPORTED blocker in a scratch copy and see
  what is actually behind it. Three of the four rows were wrong.

  ✔ REAL — ripple_carry_adder / tuple-returning helper. Inlining the tuple
    (`let ab = a[i]^b[i]; sum_bits[i] = ab^carry; carry = …`) makes the module
    transpile outright. The recorded cause is the only cause.

  ✘ MASKING — uart_tx, uart_rx were filed under "no structural parent module".
    STILL THE LIVE CAUSE, re-verified 2026-08-25: both still fail at FILE level on
    `spawn_uart`, before either module is looked at. That row conflates two
    different things:
      - transpiling the two LEAF modules, which the parent has nothing to do
        with. They failed only because `spawn_uart` has a hardware-looking
        signature with no `#[hardware]`, which `prepare_source` rejects at FILE
        level before any module is looked at. With `spawn_uart` removed from the
        file they get further, and fail on their OWN content (below).
      - transpiling the SYSTEM as a hierarchy, which is a genuine separate
        feature and is what the row was presumably about.
    Keep them separate; the leaf modules are not blocked on hierarchy.

  ◐ UNDERSTATED — **now filed as cause M**, which supersedes this note; kept for
    the measurement. The UART family (uart_tx, uart_rx, uart/rx) is blocked on
    "a tick inside a `for` loop", which is BROADER than the recorded "counted
    delay". Both `for _ in 0..CLKS_PER_BIT { tick }` (bit timing) AND
    `for i in 0..8 { … tick … }` (the data-bit loop) are instances — removing
    only the former leaves the module rejected. The class is counted REPETITION,
    not just delay, so a fix has to cover a loop whose body does work as well as
    one that only waits.
    NOTE the reported error names the wrong construct: control extraction
    declines because of the `for`, and the linear path downstream then reports
    the desugared `while` as a nested-loop ordering problem. Same
    error-message-is-not-the-cause pattern, now visible INSIDE one module.

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — CONTROL EXTRACTION DROPPED A LOCAL
    THAT LIVES ACROSS A TICK. Found while re-measuring uart_tx, minimal repro:
        loop {
            while go.read() == Zero { clk.tick().await; }   // forces extraction
            let captured = d.read();
            clk.tick().await;
            o.write(captured);                              // undefined variable
            clk.tick().await;
        }
    → "undefined variable 'captured' referenced in hardware expression".
    The SAME body without the wait — so without extraction — transpiles fine and
    `captured` becomes a register, which is the core of the whole timing model
    ("every value live across an await becomes a register"). So the two paths
    disagree about the defining rule of the language.
    Not silent: it is a clean error, and the emitted-vs-dropped question never
    arises. But the diagnostic is poor (the variable is plainly defined two lines
    up), and this is a hard blocker for any extracted body that captures a value
    — which is most real sequential designs, not just the UART.
    FIX: `hoist_cross_state_locals` moves a local DEFINED in one state and READ in
    another out to a pre-loop `let mut`, leaving a plain assignment where the
    `let` was — the same treatment `pc` itself gets, so it lands on the existing
    register path rather than a new one. A local read only within its own state
    stays put; hoisting every local would turn state-local temporaries into
    flip-flops nobody asked for.
    Verified: tests/cross_state_local_equivalence.rs (sim ≡ transpiled SV under
    Verilator, with `d` changing BETWEEN the capture and the read so a value that
    tracked instead of holding would diverge, plus an independent assertion that
    every value the output takes is one that was actually captured).
    CONFIRMS THE RE-MEASUREMENT: with this fixed, uart_tx transpiles in full once
    its counted delays are removed by hand — so the counted delay really is its
    last remaining blocker, which could not be established before.

  ◐ STALE — rv32i_cpu / rv32i_cpu_pipelined. The first blocker is still the
    `Vec<Bits<32>>` port, confirmed. The four-item chain recorded behind it was
    measured 2026-08-24 BEFORE D-a, I, D-b, J-a and C-a landed, so items 2-4 are
    hypotheses now, not measurements. Re-measure before acting on them. The
    conclusion that a synthesizable CPU is a REWRITE rather than a flip does not
    depend on those items and still stands.

  ── COUNTED DELAYS: ATTEMPTED, REVERTED, DIAGNOSED (2026-08-24) ─────────────
  `for … in 0..N { … clk.tick().await; }` is N CLOCK CYCLES, not a repetition to
  unroll (a UART bit period is 434). Attempted as a DESUGAR rather than a new
  state shape, rewriting it into the repeating-wait form control extraction
  already flattens:
      let mut ctr = <start>;
      loop {
          if ctr == <end> { break; }      // exit test at the TOP
          <body without its trailing tick>
          ctr = ctr + 1;
          clk.tick().await;
      }
  The test has to be at the top: the body then runs for ctr = start..end-1 and
  ticks once per iteration, then re-enters, sees `ctr == end` and breaks in the
  SAME cycle (break is not a clock boundary). Testing after the tick would both
  lose a tick and put the test in the window the language refuses.

  REVERTED — it transpiles but is WRONG, and the equivalence tests written for it
  caught the divergence (sim said 6, Verilator said 5). Diagnosis, from the
  emitted FSM for a 3-cycle delay:
      8'd0: __copper_ctr0 <= ((32'd0 == 32'd3) ? …);   // folded to the literal
      8'd0: if ((__copper_ctr0 == 32'd3)) o <= n;      // NOT folded — reads the reg
  The entry state carries BOTH a literal-substituted copy of the loop test and a
  register-reading copy of it. Entering must not cost a cycle, so the flattener
  clones the lowered head into the entry point (the same trick the wait uses) —
  but the counter's first value is only knowable as a literal there, since the
  register does not commit until the edge. One of the two copies got the literal
  and the other did not, and the register is uninitialised at that point.

  ✘ THAT DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG, and knowing why matters more than the bug did.
  It reads as "entry specialisation" — a problem with how the desugar and the
  clone-into-entry trick interact. It is not. The two lines above come from two
  DIFFERENT code paths in `shir_lower`: the register update from
  `extract_updates_from_stmts`, which forwards a segment's own assignments, and
  the `if` condition from `lower_stmt_list`, which did not forward at all. The
  entry state was not special; NO branch condition anywhere read a register its
  own segment had just assigned. That is cause N (2026-08-25), it is reachable
  with no `for` in sight — `c = c + 1; if c == 3 { … }` — and with the counter
  also reset inside the branch it silently produced a dead output.
  With N fixed the entry test reads `<start>` with no special casing, and the
  degenerate `0..0` falls out: the test is at the top, so the loop breaks before
  ticking. The real remaining problem was somewhere else entirely (the wasted
  back edge — see cause M).
  RECORD THE CLASS: a diagnosis inferred from the SHAPE of the emitted code
  ("one copy got the literal and the other did not") named the pass it was
  looking at rather than the pass that produced each copy. Both copies were
  wrong-by-construction from one missing call, and the fix was five lines in a
  stage the note never mentions.

  ✔ RESOLVED (2026-08-25) — this entry said counted delays "AND IT IS NOT
    SUFFICIENT ANYWAY", because each UART loop's body ENDS IN another tick-bearing
    loop:
        for i in 0..8 { tx_serial.write(byte[i]); for _ in 0..CLKS_PER_BIT { tick } }
    That was cause K, and it is FIXED — `body_ends_at_a_clock_boundary` admits the
    shape, and the diagnostic complaint recorded here is fixed too
    (`chir_lower::nested_loop_error`).
    THE ORDER OF WORK RECORDED HERE WAS BACKWARDS, and the reason is structural
    rather than a mis-estimate. It said: nested-loop-tail FIRST, counted delays
    second, "doing them the other way round unblocks nothing". In fact K on its
    own unblocks nothing EITHER, and cannot: the tick-last rule means every
    hand-written inner `loop` can exit having ticked zero times, so an outer loop
    whose only boundary is one of them is a zero-time cycle that
    `check_reachability` now rejects. The example above — with a `for` inside — is
    the FIRST writable instance of the shape. Counted repetition is therefore the
    prerequisite, not the sequel. See cause M, which is now the next piece of work.

  ✔ KEPT from the attempt — `hoist_cross_state_locals` dropped the TYPE
    ANNOTATION when hoisting (`ty: None`), so a hoisted local whose init has no
    inferable width of its own (a bare `0`) failed. Latent in the committed hoist;
    fixed independently of the reverted work.

  ── LINT-CLEAN IS NOT THE SAME AS TRANSPILES ────────────────────────────────
  A module can pass the CLI and still fail the equivalence harness, which runs
  Verilator under `-Wall`. TWO modules unblocked above are in exactly that state,
  both for the same reason — a bare `usize` local:
    bsg_encode_one_hot (D-a): `let mut addr = 0usize;` emits `logic [63:0] addr`;
      the 3-bit `assign addr_o = addr` is WIDTHTRUNC, plus UNUSEDSIGNAL on
      `addr[63:3]`.
    bsg_counter_up_down (I): `let mut count: usize = 0;` emits `logic [31:0]`;
      `assign count_o = count` is WIDTHTRUNC, and `up = (up_i == 1'b1)` is
      WIDTHEXPAND (a 1-bit RHS into a 32-bit target).
  ✔ THE WIDTH DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN THEM IS FIXED (2026-08-24). It read 64 bits
  for `0usize` and 32 for `: usize = 0` — two spellings of one Rust type reaching
  different inference paths. `usize` gets a width in THREE places in chir_lower:
  `resolve_type` (:127, the annotation path), `infer_type_from_suffix` (:969) and
  `strip_int_suffix` (:3675, both the literal-suffix path). History settles which
  was intended: both 64s are first-draft code (d48dcb7, 5a55011), while the 32
  was a deliberate later decision (78d91f7, 2026-07-24 — "matches SV `int` loop
  var, keeps index arithmetic width-consistent"), and that commit also added
  `from_usize` → 32 to mirror it. It updated the TYPE table and missed the two
  LITERAL tables for the same type. Now aligned at 32, with `isize` added to both
  (it was in resolve_type at 32 and absent from the suffix tables entirely).
  Worse than cosmetic while it lasted: nothing stopped the two meeting in one
  expression, where the mismatch is a silent width conversion, not an error.
  Pinned by transpile_inference_gaps.rs::both_spellings_of_usize_give_the_same_width.

  ✔ BOTH MODULES NOW LINT AND HAVE EQUIVALENCE TESTS (2026-08-24, user's call).
  The `usize` alignment above did NOT unblock them — measured, both still failed
  `-Wall`, just at 32 bits instead of 64/32, because a wide integer local driving
  a NARROW port is a separate blocker independent of what `usize` resolves to.
  Resolved by narrowing the examples rather than by inference: `usize` is not a
  hardware type, and an accumulator typed at its real width is what the design
  means. `bsg_encode_one_hot`'s `addr` → `Bits<ADDR_W>`; `bsg_counter_up_down`'s
  `count` → `Bits<PTR_W>`, which also let the `(count + MOD - down + up) % MOD`
  dance go — that existed only to keep a `usize` from underflowing, and `Bits<3>`
  wraps on its own. What is left is BaseJump's own recurrence, `count - down + up`.
  Both still pass their sim ≡ BaseJump self-check, and both gained
  sim ≡ transpiled-SV: tests/bsg_encode_one_hot_equivalence.rs and
  tests/bsg_counter_up_down_equivalence.rs, `include!`ing the example rather than
  copying it, so the two checks cannot drift (same structure as sipo_block).
  That chains the anchors — the GENERATED SystemVerilog is now transitively tied
  to hardware neither Copper nor its transpiler wrote.

  ☐ STILL OPEN, and now the standing question rather than a blocker: should a bare
  integer local get its width from its TYPE (today: `usize` → 32) or from its USES?
  Nothing in the corpus needs the answer any more, so this is a language-design
  call, not a bug. J-a sharpened it rather than settling it: a 32-bit integer
  local used purely as an INDEX into a narrow vector lowers correctly but draws a
  Verilator `UNUSEDSIGNAL` on its upper bits (measured on `let k = WIDTH - 1;`
  indexing a `Bits<8>` — `k[31:3]` unused). Verilator's own `for (int k = …)` does
  not trip this because a loop variable is declared inside the block, not as a
  module-level signal. A context-derived width would fix that class; narrowing at
  the source (what the BaseJump accumulators did) sidesteps it. (The related footgun found while narrowing — `Bits::one()`
  returning ALL ONES rather than the value 1 — is ✔ FIXED: renamed `all_ones()`,
  2026-08-24. `Bits::zero()` reads as the value 0 and IS 0, so the pair invited
  exactly the wrong guess; `Bits::from_lit::<1>()` is the value. Hard rename, no
  deprecated alias — leaving `one()` reachable would leave the trap reachable.)
  The transpile count above is "the CLI emits SystemVerilog", not "it lints".

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — D-b: array-typed ports
    (`In<[Bits<W>; ELS], D>`) had no lowering. Chosen ABI: a **packed 2-D**
    SystemVerilog vector, `[ELS-1:0][W-1:0]`. Full option analysis with the
    measurements in design_docs/ARRAY_PORT_ABI.md; the three reasons, in order:
      1. Both independent BaseJump references declare exactly that, so a Copper
         module stays drop-in interchangeable with the hardware it is checked
         against.
      2. It needs NO width arithmetic. `Width` is `Concrete | Param` with no
         operators, and BOTH blocked modules are symbolic in both dimensions
         (`mux` is generic; `bsg_mux_one_hot`'s consts are localparams), so the
         flat `[ELS*W-1:0]` alternative could not have avoided adding a product
         variant — ~11 match sites plus the exhaustiveness errors behind them.
      3. Indexing stays a direct element select, so the body reads like the
         reference instead of an indexed part-select.
    MEASURED FIRST, and it is what collapsed the decision: Verilator gives packed
    2-D and flat 1-D the IDENTICAL C++ interface (`VL_IN(&x,31,0)` for both), so
    the two are bit-identical and harness-identical — the testbench generator
    needed no change, and the "element 0 at the LSBs" flattening the mux example
    already used stays correct. The same measurement rules OUT an unpacked array,
    which appears in C++ as a member array that `top->d = value` cannot assign.
    ARRAY LOCALS NEEDED THE SAME SHAPE, and this is the bug worth remembering:
    `let d = data_i.read();` was declared at the ELEMENT width, so the array
    silently truncated into one element and every `d[i]` became a bit-select. The
    emitted module compiled and was wrong. Found on bsg_mux_one_hot; the outer
    dimension is now carried on wire declarations too, pinned by
    array_ports.rs::a_local_holding_an_array_keeps_both_dimensions.
    ALSO FIXED IN PASSING: a bare literal compared against a module parameter was
    sized at the 64-bit default (`ELS_P == 64'd1`), a WIDTHEXPAND error under
    `-Wall`. `balance_binop_literals` existed for exactly this but could not see a
    parameter's width, since a parameter is not a signal. Parameters and
    localparams emit as `int`, so they are 32 bits — consistent with the `usize`
    resolution and the SV `int` loop variable.
    Both examples' accumulators were also narrowed to `Bits<N>` (same reason as
    bsg_encode_one_hot/bsg_counter_up_down), which for the one-hot module also
    replaced `acc |= …` with the reference's own mask-and-OR shape.
    Tests: copper-codegen/tests/array_ports.rs (5 — the concrete declaration, the
    symbolic one with an assertion that NO width product appears, direct-index
    emission, the array-local declaration, and nested arrays refused) plus
    tests/mux_equivalence.rs (dynamic index, symbolic dimensions) and
    tests/bsg_mux_one_hot_equivalence.rs (constant index, array local). The pinned
    unsupported_constructs.rs::array_typed_port_is_unsupported was removed, as its
    own message demanded: promoted to positive coverage, not relaxed.

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — I: a branch-local `let` with a
    COMPUTED initializer was reported as a latch. Minimal repro:
      if r { count = 0 } else { let up = up_i.read(); if up == One { … } }
    → "would infer a latch: up assigned on some control paths but not all".
    `up` is written and read entirely inside the one branch — Rust SCOPES it
    there, so nothing outside can observe it and it cannot latch.
    `vlir_lower::hoist_branch_local_defaults` already handled exactly this shape,
    but only when the initializer was a LITERAL: it moved the whole assignment to
    the top, which is sound only for a constant. A computed initializer cannot
    move (it would be evaluated on paths the source never runs it on), so it now
    leaves a zero DEFAULT at the top and stays where it was written.
    THE RULE IS ONLY SAFE BECAUSE OF WHAT IT LEAVES ALONE. A conditionally-driven
    REGISTER is indistinguishable from a latch by the "not assigned on all paths"
    test, but it is the implicit-HOLD idiom (verified against BaseJump's
    `bsg_dff_en` — see the `Out` holds-when-unwritten entry). An unconditional
    default would clear it on every untaken path: a silent, cycle-accurate wrong
    answer rather than a compile error. Registers and registered outputs are
    therefore passed in as an explicit exempt set rather than being distinguished
    by inspection, and a test pins that they never receive a blocking default.
    Tests: `copper-codegen/tests/branch_local_defaults.rs` (3 — the default plus
    kept computation, the register-never-defaulted invariant, and the unchanged
    literal path) and `tests/branch_local_temp_equivalence.rs` (sim ≡ transpiled
    SV under Verilator for a DUT that carries BOTH shapes at once, with hold
    cycles in the stimulus that would fail if the register were defaulted).

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — D-a: file-scope `const` items had
    no lowering. `const WIDTH: usize = 8;` sitting next to a module is visible to
    that module's body and port types but is not reachable from the `ItemFn`, so
    the transpiler reported `undefined variable 'WIDTH'`. `capture_file_scope`
    had been collecting these into `FrontendModuleIR::file_consts` all along —
    its own doc said "Nothing consumes the result yet" — so the fix consumes an
    existing capture path rather than adding one, alongside the file-scope ENUM
    injection that already worked the same way.
    FIRST, THE EARLIER FRAMING WAS WRONG. This entry used to read "generic const
    params in loop bounds". It is not that: `shift_register` is generic over
    `const N` and has always transpiled, emitting `parameter int N` and using it
    in loop bounds AND indices. Two of the four modules filed under D were file
    consts; the other two are array ports, which is a different problem (D-b).
    THREE DECISIONS, each measured rather than argued:
      - `localparam`, NOT `parameter`. A Rust `const` is a fixed value, not a
        knob; an overridable parameter would let a synthesized module elaborate
        at a width no Copper simulation ever ran.
      - IN THE PARAMETER PORT LIST, not the module body. A const can appear in a
        port width (`In<Bits<WIDTH>, D>` → `[WIDTH-1:0]`), and a body declaration
        is not in scope there. SystemVerilog permits `local_parameter_declaration`
        inside a `parameter_port_list` — VERIFIED lint-clean against Verilator
        5.044 before writing any code, because the whole design hinged on it.
      - THE SOURCE EXPRESSION IS PRESERVED, not evaluated: `const MOD = 1 << PTR_W`
        emits `localparam int MOD = 1 << PTR_W`, and `PTR_W` is pulled in with it,
        ahead of it (SV resolves the list left to right). No const evaluator is
        needed — SystemVerilog evaluates it.
    THE SET MUST BE EXACT, NOT A SUPERSET. Verilator's UNUSEDPARAM makes an
    unreferenced `localparam` a hard error under `-Wall` (measured; this is why
    "just emit every const in the file" is not an option). So the emitted set is
    computed at emission from the RENDERED port list + body, closed transitively
    over the initializers. Deriving it from the IR instead would mean a second
    traversal that has to stay in step with every future statement and expression
    variant — the "a check that cannot see a construct reports green on it" class
    this file already records twice.
    A const with no SystemVerilog form (a `const fn` call — `mux.rs`'s
    `safe_clog2(ELS_P)` — or a non-integer type) is NOT emitted, and referencing
    it stays the ordinary "undefined variable" error with the reason appended, so
    it does not read as a typo.
    Code: `copper-codegen/src/file_consts.rs` (new), threaded CHIR→SHIR→VLIR as
    `localparams`, emitted by `emit::emit_param_header`.
    Tests: `copper-codegen/tests/file_scope_consts.rs` (7 — port width + loop
    bound, localparam-not-parameter, unused-not-emitted, dependency order and
    expression preservation, both rejection diagnostics, and the substring guard
    that keeps `WIDTH` out when only `WIDTH_P` is used) and
    `tests/file_scope_const_equivalence.rs` (sim ≡ transpiled SV under Verilator
    for a const-parameterised one-hot encoder, against an independently written
    reference model).

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24) — A: `[Logic::Zero; N]` array locals.
    An array local now lowers to the packed vector its repeat length describes —
    element k at bit k, the SAME layout Bits<N> already has in copper-core
    (`Bits { bits: [Logic; N] }`). Because the layouts coincide,
    `Bits::from_slice`/`from_array` move no bits and lower to IDENTITY.
      copper-codegen/src/chir_lower.rs: `ExprType::Repeat` arm in
      infer_type_from_expr (+ `repeat_len`, `chir_type_width`), `lower_repeat`,
      and `identity_pack_call`.
      Symbolic lengths work (`[Logic::Zero; N]` → `logic [N-1:0]`): repeating a
      ZERO is zero at any width, so it emits as a context-width literal the way
      Bits::zero() does. `[Bits<8>; N]` stays a clean error — Width has no product
      form, so it is refused rather than silently mis-sized.
      VERIFIED BEHAVIORALLY, not by shape: tests/logic_array_pack_equivalence.rs
      (+ fixtures/logic_array_pack_dut.rs) — sim ≡ transpiled SV ≡ an INDEPENDENT
      Rust golden under Verilator, sweeping all 16 values of each nibble field.
      copper-codegen/tests/transpile_inference_gaps.rs: the array half is now a
      POSITIVE assertion (per that file's own instruction — promote, do not relax).

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24) — B: fully-qualified type paths.
    Every port/clock test in chir_lower.rs is TEXTUAL (`starts_with("Clock<")`,
    `"In<"`, `"Out<"`), so `copper_core::Clock<MainClk>` failed all of them and
    was reported unresolvable. sipo_block was the one example written that way.
      New `compact_type()` / `strip_path_qualifier()` choke point; the 9 type-text
      compaction sites in chir_lower.rs + the one in parser.rs route through it.
      Only the HEAD path is stripped, so a qualifier inside a generic argument
      (`In<Bits<8>, some_mod::Dom>`) is untouched. Literal, path and pattern texts
      deliberately do NOT go through it — a qualifier is meaningful there
      (`Logic::One`, `Opcode::LUI`).

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — E: `Memory` had NO transpiled path.
    Not one construct being rejected — an entire first-class feature that existed
    only in the simulator. The IRs had no memory node at all (`grep -i memory`
    over chir/shir/vlir/frontend_ir: zero hits), so every memory guarantee was
    sim-only and the central claim (one source simulates AND synthesises, provably
    in agreement) did not extend to any design using memory.
      SCOPE, chosen with the user: single-cycle (READ_LAT = WRITE_LAT = 1)
      ReadFirst, N read + M write ports, no preload. Everything else is a CLEAN
      error naming the construct — never a silent mis-lowering — and every one of
      those boundaries is pinned in copper-codegen/tests/unsupported_constructs.rs
      against a baseline DUT that DOES transpile, so a pin can only fail for the
      construct it names.
      THE LOWERING IS MECHANICAL because the sim API is already the hardware
      staging pattern (this is why the OUTDATED MEMORY_DESIGN.md indexing API was
      NOT adopted — the transpiler lowers the API the examples actually use):
        Memory::<T,R,W,D,1,1>::new(clk, N)  → logic [W-1:0] mem [0:N-1];
        pre-tick  wp::<j>().write(a,d)      → if (en) mem[a] <= d;   in always_ff
        pre-tick  rp::<i>().read(a)         → en/addr comb nets
        post-tick rp::<i>().data()          → assign rd_data = mem[rd_addr];
        post-tick rp::<i>().is_ready()      → the enable net (LAT=1: ready ⟺ staged)
      ReadFirst falls out of non-blocking assignment for free — the continuous
      read sees the array before the edge commits the write — which is exactly
      what the hand-written reference does (`if (enb) dob <= ram[addrb];`).
      IR: CHIRMemoryDecl + CHIRStmt::MemRead/MemWrite + CHIRExpr::MemData/MemValid,
      mirrored through SHIR, into VLIRMemDecl + VLIRFFStmt::MemAssign. The net
      names are reserved in the legalizer from the DECLARATION, so a user signal
      cannot collide with a synthesized one.
      WHY THE NETS ARE COMB WIRES, not registers: the post-tick `data()` read
      observes what the edge PRODUCED. Lowering it to a pipeline register would
      read the register's pre-edge value inside always_ff and lag the design by a
      cycle. Feeding the register update from a continuous read of the array
      collapses to `if (en) q <= mem[addr]` — the reference's own shape.
      VERIFIED BEHAVIOURALLY: tests/dual_port_ram_equivalence.rs — sim ≡ transpiled
      SV under Verilator over 11 cycles including a deliberate read-during-write at
      the same address (the one cycle where a wrong lowering is otherwise
      invisible), PLUS a shape assertion, PLUS an independent Rust reference model.
      The example's own sim ≡ hand-written-SV check still passes, so the generated
      SV is transitively anchored to a memory neither Copper nor its transpiler
      wrote. Example `include!`d, not copied — one source of truth.
      ALSO REMOVED: dual_port_ram declared a `data_out_a` output it never drove.
      Harmless in simulation, a Verilator UNDRIVEN error once it transpiled. The
      hand-written reference has no `doa` either (it is commented out), so the
      interfaces now match. NOTE the open language question this exposes: Copper
      does not reject an undriven `Out` at compile time — the transpiler found it.

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24) — G: the transpiler PANICKED on `examples/cpu/rv32i_cpu.rs`.
      thread 'main' panicked at control_extract.rs:95: gate guarantees at least one tick
    ROOT CAUSE: the two halves of control extraction disagreed about where a
    `clk.tick().await` can live. The GATE (`expr_contains_tick`) descends into
    `loop` / `while` / `for`; the FLATTENER's search (`find_tick_in_expr`) descends
    only into `if` / `block` / `match`. A module whose tick sits inside a nested
    `loop` — the "wait until ready" idiom the CPU uses ~15 times — passed the gate
    and then hit an `.expect`. No span, no construct named, nothing to act on.
    FIX, in three parts:
      1. The gate now also requires that every tick sit somewhere the flattener can
         actually handle (`contains_unflattenable_tick`). A tick under a repeating
         construct means DECLINE — leave the module alone so the linear path
         downstream reports the real construct with its own span. Note the
         flattener was never going to handle these: `lower_into` has no `loop` case
         at all, so had the panic not fired it would have embedded a nested
         ticking loop inside a state body.
      2. The `.expect` became a graceful bail, as defence in depth: if the two
         halves ever drift apart again the pass declines instead of crashing.
      3. chir_lower gained real diagnostics for the loop family. A nested `loop`
         used to fall through to a generic handler that printed
         `0:0: expression type not supported in hardware: Discriminant(14)` — no
         span, no name. Now: a nested loop WITH a tick says it is a repeating wait
         and that control extraction does not build the self-looping state yet; one
         WITHOUT a tick says it would never terminate; `break`/`continue` are named
         too. `ExprType` gained `kind_name()` and `span()` so the generic
         fall-through names the construct and points at it instead of printing a
         discriminant.
    GUARD: copper-codegen/tests/no_transpiler_panics.rs sweeps every #[hardware]
    module in examples/ and asserts each one transpiles OR returns a clean Err —
    a panic is the failure MODE this pins, not a coverage claim. Verified by
    reverting the fix: the guard failed naming examples/cpu/rv32i_cpu.rs::rv32i_cpu.
    Construct-level pins in unsupported_constructs.rs cover the nested-loop shapes,
    including which check owns which (the shared REACHABILITY guarantee owns a
    module with no tick at all, and its message is the better one).

  ✔ A+B UNBLOCKED sipo_block THROUGH THE FRONT END, WHICH IMMEDIATELY EXPOSED THE
     multi-phase RegOut codegen bug — and, verifying that, a second one in the
     same family for plain `Out`. BOTH now FIXED (see above). sipo_block is now
     sim ≡ transpiled SV ≡ BaseJump on all five outputs.

  ☐ BUG/GAP (found 2026-08-03 while writing equivalence tests), HALF FIXED:
    the standalone `transpile_source` path rejected two constructs the macro-sim
    accepts, both with "cannot infer bit width":
      - `[Logic::Zero; N]` array locals — ✔ FIXED 2026-08-24, see A above
      - tuple-returning plain-Rust helper fns called from hardware bodies
        (e.g. `let (s, c) = full_adder(...)`; inlining the expression works)
        → STILL OPEN, still pinned in transpile_inference_gaps.rs
    examples/combinational/ripple_carry_adder.rs still does NOT round-trip — it
    is blocked on the TUPLE half, not the array half (measured: with A fixed it
    now fails at the `let (s, c) = full_adder(...)` line). Its equivalence test
    keeps the Bits-indexing rewrite until the tuple gap closes.
  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-03): Moore output decoded from a state `case` was lowered into
    `always_ff` (REGISTERED, `out <= …`) instead of `always_comb`, lagging the
    simulator one cycle (verilator FAIL / trace PASS).
      TRUE ROOT CAUSE (my first "single-register" hypothesis was wrong): the trigger
      is a NON-EXHAUSTIVE state `case` — a register whose encoding leaves unreachable
      values (6 states in a 3-bit reg; a `u8` pc with 2 states). ports_driven_all_paths
      refused to count ANY output as fully driven on a case with no default that isn't
      exhaustive, so an output written on every explicit arm still looked "conditional"
      and got moved to always_ff. traffic_light is immune only because 4 states = 2^2
      is exactly exhaustive. (Nothing to do with register count.)
      FIX: copper-codegen/src/vlir_lower.rs `hoist_moore_output_defaults` — for an
      output that is currently conditional AND driven on every explicit arm, hoist a
      top-of-`always_comb` default (`out = <first-arm value>;`) and give the case an
      empty `default` (Verilator CASEINCOMPLETE is an error). Narrow by design:
      exhaustive cases (traffic_light) are byte-identical; a genuine enabled-register
      hold driven in only some arms (bsg_dff_en) is untouched and stays `always_ff`
      (both verified). seq6 → full equivalence (tests/fsm_six_state_equivalence.rs).
      RESIDUAL (separate, still open): see the Mealy-output item directly below.
  ✔ RESOLVED-BY-RESTRICTION (entry was STALE, corrected 2026-08-24): the MEALY
    output sim-vs-SV divergence flagged 2026-08-03 is no longer open, but note it
    was NOT fixed — it was ruled out of the language. The multi-write-around-a-tick
    guardrail rejects a plain (combinational) `Out` in that shape and points at
    `RegOut`, which reconciles with the transpiled `always_ff`. So the divergent
    program can no longer be written, rather than the divergence being repaired.
      EVIDENCE the entry was stale: tests/nested_tick_equivalence.rs and
      tests/fsm_six_state_equivalence.rs both run FULL sim ≡ transpiled-SV
      equivalence now; the only `sim_only` occurrences left in either file are
      historical comments. This entry still said "Meanwhile ... runs sim_only".
      PAPER NOTE: cite this as a scope restriction, not a repair — it belongs with
      contribution 5 (the `Out`/`RegOut` pair), not with the Moore-output fix.

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — RegOut written UNCONDITIONALLY in a
    multi-phase loop was emitted as a continuous `assign`, not a flip-flop.
      ROOT CAUSE: vlir_lower::lower_flat_stmts turned EVERY top-level
      SHIRStmt::PortDrive into a module-level VLIRContinuousAssign. Those assigns
      are collected separately from the phase's statements and never passed
      through split_output_regs — so the port's `registered` flag was silently
      ignored for unconditional writes, and the phase guard was lost with it. The
      output then followed a phase-gated combinational value and collapsed to 0
      on the cycles where that phase was inactive.
      WHY mac_fsm WAS ALWAYS FINE: it writes its RegOut INSIDE a conditional,
      which lowers to a PortAssign in the phase statements — which
      split_output_regs does see. Only the unconditional shape was broken.
      FIX: one arm in lower_flat_stmts — a PortDrive targeting a registered
      output becomes a PortAssign instead of a continuous assign, so the existing
      split moves it into always_ff under the phase guard. lower_flat_stmts now
      takes `registered_outs`; the combinational caller passes an empty set (a
      comb module has no clock, so no registered outputs).
      THE SIM WAS THE CORRECT SIDE: it matches BaseJump's own sipo_block.sv,
      which registers data_o in an always_ff.
      REGRESSION COVER: tests/regout_multiphase_equivalence.rs — a behavioural
      sim ≡ SV check PLUS a shape assertion (`word_o <=` present, `assign word_o`
      absent), because the behavioural check alone could pass by luck on the
      sampled cycles.
      NEW COVERAGE UNLOCKED: tests/sipo_block_equivalence.rs — sipo_block now has
      sim ≡ transpiled SV on top of the example's existing sim ≡ BaseJump check,
      so the generated SV is transitively anchored to third-party hardware. It
      `include!`s the example (with #[cfg(not(test))] on its main) rather than
      copying it, so there is one source of truth. All five outputs compared once
      the plain-Out fix below landed.

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — a PLAIN `Out` written in only SOME
    PHASES of a multi-phase loop was emitted as an unguarded continuous assign, so
    it dropped to 0 outside its phase instead of holding.
      SAME MECHANISM as the RegOut bug above (top-level PortDrive → unguarded
      continuous assign over a phase-gated wire defaulting to '0), different port
      kind. Sim gave 1,1,3,3; the SV gave 0,2,0,4.
      THE SIM WAS THE CORRECT SIDE: a sequential plain `Out` HOLDS when unwritten
      (the enabled-register idiom, verified against BaseJump's bsg_dff_en), so a
      write in one phase is EFFECTIVELY conditional and must become an
      implicit-hold register — the same treatment a write on some-but-not-all
      PATHS already got.
      WHY THE RegOut PATCH ALONE WAS NOT ENOUGH: conditional_output_ports decides
      conditionality by finding writes nested in a conditional. A phase-top-level
      write is unconditional WITHIN its phase, so routing it to a PortAssign left
      it unrecognised. lower_seq therefore also computes
      `phase_scoped_output_ports` from the SHIR. It has to be done at SHIR level:
      the VLIR-level ports_driven_any_path cannot see an unconditional drive,
      because that has already been moved out of the statement list. The two sets
      are unioned into one `hold_outs`, so RegOut and phase-scoped plain Out share
      one mechanism.
      ⚠️ THE RULE KEYS ON THE DRIVEN VALUE, NOT ON PHASE COVERAGE — AND THE FIRST
      ATTEMPT GOT THIS WRONG. "Written in some phases but not all" LAGGED
      mac_pipeline's output by one cycle (expected 10 got 0, expected 37 got 10);
      caught by the full regression, tests/mac_pipeline_equivalence.rs is the
      measured witness. A top-level drive lowers to an UNGUARDED continuous
      assign, so it reads its RHS every cycle; correctness depends on what the RHS
      is OUTSIDE the writing phase:
        - mac_pipeline: `out.write(sum)` reads the inferred REGISTER sum_r, which
          RETAINS its value → `assign out = sum_r` is already correct, and
          registering it again just adds a cycle.
        - sipo_block / the fixture: the drive reads a PHASE-LOCAL COMBINATIONAL
          WIRE that always_comb defaults to '0 → the assign propagates zeros.
      Only the second needs the hold. Helpers: shir_expr_vars,
      shir_phase_local_wires, shir_port_drives.
      DELIBERATELY NARROW ON TWO COUNTS: a port written in EVERY phase is excluded
      (driven every cycle), and so is a port whose value is register-backed.
      Widening either lags an output by a cycle — do not widen without a measured
      witness.
      REGRESSION COVER: tests/plain_out_multiphase_equivalence.rs — behavioural
      sim ≡ SV plus a shape assertion (`seen_o <=` present, `assign seen_o`
      absent).
      COVERAGE UNLOCKED: tests/sipo_block_equivalence.rs now compares ALL FIVE
      outputs (data_o + the four wN_dbg), not just data_o. The wN_dbg ports expose
      the module's per-cycle internal capture, which is exactly what the
      mid-phase-read question sipo_block was built to answer.

  ✔ CONTROL EXTRACTION INCREMENT B — repeating waits  [DONE 2026-08-24]
     `loop { <test>; clk.tick().await; }` nested inside the module's own loop, left
     by `break`. Increment A flattened ticks nested in `if`/`match` by inlining each
     continuation into the branches; a nested loop adds the one thing that model
     lacked — a state that loops back to ITSELF.
     THE LOWERING: the loop gets a head state H; its body is lowered once with H as
     the back-edge target, so the body's trailing tick emits `pc = H` (stay another
     cycle) and `break` INLINES the loop's continuation (leave, in the same cycle —
     breaking is not a clock boundary). Entering must not cost a cycle either, so
     the already-lowered body is CLONED into the entry point. Cloning the lowered
     form rather than re-lowering the source is what keeps it cheap: the two copies
     share the sub-states their ticks allocated, so a wait costs ONE extra state,
     not a doubling. (The state-count worry recorded when this was proposed turned
     out not to bite, for that reason.)
     `if ready { break; }` also forces a split increment A would not have made: the
     branch holds no tick, but control still leaves it, so the continuation must be
     inlined per-branch anyway. "Contains a tick" became "diverges" as the splitting
     rule.
     → tests/wait_loop_equivalence.rs (4 tests): a single wait, TWO SEQUENTIAL
       waits with a tick between them, a shape pin that one wait costs one extra
       state, and the refused ordering. Stimulus uses uneven runs (2, 1, 3 cycles)
       so a wait that held for a FIXED number of cycles could not pass.
     BOTH NEGATIVE CONTROLS MEASURED: making the back edge target state 0 instead of
     the loop head broke the two-wait handshake (a single wait cannot tell the two
     apart — which is why the handshake test exists); making `break` jump to a new
     state instead of inlining broke all three.

  ── THE MID-PHASE READ SEAM, MEASURED 2026-08-24 ────────────────────────────
  Went looking for one thing and found three. The refusal of
  `loop { clk.tick().await; <test> }` was blamed on a read-timing seam; that blame
  was PARTLY WRONG, and taking it apart turned up a silent miscompilation that had
  nothing to do with control flow.

  ✔ FIXED — A REGISTER REWRITTEN INSIDE ONE BRANCH LOST EVERY WRITE BUT THE FIRST.
     `shir_lower::extract_updates_from_stmts` merges a branch's register updates
     into one muxed next-value, and looked each register's value up with `.find()`
     — the FIRST update in the branch. A register written twice kept the earlier
     write:
         t <= (t + 4'd1);          // emitted
         //   `if t == 3 { t = 0; }` — GONE
     That is the mod-N counter idiom, i.e. how anyone writes a clock divider, and
     it silently produced a free-running counter that wrapped at its full width.
     Silent because the surviving value is perfectly well-typed.
     WHY IT SURVIVED THIS LONG: outside a branch the merge does not happen — the
     updates emit as separate non-blocking assignments and SV's last-write-wins
     makes the result right anyway. So it needed a register rewritten INSIDE an
     `if` or a `match` arm, and nothing in the corpus had one: every FSM arm here
     assigns each register once.
     FIX: `last_update_for` — take the branch's LAST assignment. One line, plus a
     name and a comment so the next reader knows why order matters.
     → tests/rewritten_register_equivalence.rs (3 tests: a `match`-arm divider, a
       rewrite inside a nested `if`, and a shape pin). All three fail with the fix
       reverted; the `if` case needed the two writes in the SAME branch, since a
       top-level write plus a branch write does not reproduce it.
     RECORD THE CLASS, not just the bug: a merge that silently keeps ONE of several
     candidates keeps the wrong one for as long as nothing produces two.

  ✔ THE SEAM IS NOT ABOUT CONTROL FLOW, and it is narrower than it looked.
     With the miscompilation fixed, `loop { clk.tick().await; <test> }` is CORRECT
     when the test reads a REGISTER — measured, sim ≡ SV over a divide-by-three
     wait. So the flattening (and the body rotation it needs — the head state is
     the one the tick ENTERS) was right all along.
     What still diverges is exactly one thing: an `In` read in the post-tick window.

  ✔ THE REMAINING SEAM — CLOSED AS REFUSED BY DESIGN  [2026-08-24, user's call]
     A post-tick `In` read is sampled by the SIMULATOR at the START of the window
     (`Immediate`, per SYNCHRONOUS_SEMANTICS.md: "consumes the value the just-past
     edge produced") and by the EMITTED HARDWARE at the END of it (a flip-flop
     samples just before its own edge). Under the harness convention "drive, then
     clock", those are different input values, one cycle apart.
     NOT a "which of two adjacent samples" question: holding each stimulus value
     for TWO cycles does not reconcile them, because the two models read in
     different WINDOWS, not at different points of the same one.
     DISPOSITION: the ordering `loop { clk.tick().await; <test> }` is OUTSIDE THE
     LANGUAGE. Not "unimplemented" — decided. The supported ordering
     (`loop { <test>; clk.tick().await; }`) expresses the same designs and is what
     one would write anyway, so the restriction costs nothing that works.
     THIS IS THE SAME DISPOSITION AS D1 (pre-tick alignment): the divergent program
     is made unwritable rather than the divergence adjudicated. Copper does not
     choose between the two samplings; it declines to let a design depend on which.
     PAPER NOTE: cite as a SCOPE RESTRICTION, not a repair — alongside D1 and the
     Mealy-output item, which are the same move. The honest framing of all three is
     "Copper infers the register/combinational boundary that other HDLs make
     explicit, and where inference cannot decide, the language narrows."
     WHY THE COST IS LOW, measured: the divergence needs an input that CHANGES
     MID-CYCLE. An `In` driven by a clocked module in the same domain is stable
     across the window, so both models read the same value. It is a
     testbench-observable difference — which is exactly why it could not be left
     in, since sim ≡ SV under a testbench is this project's bar.
     REJECTED ALTERNATIVES, recorded so they are not re-tried:
       - register the input at the entering edge so the hardware reproduces the
         simulator. Exact under any stimulus, and it would have allowed the
         ordering — but it silently inserts a flip-flop the user did not write and
         delays the input a cycle relative to the plain reading of the source.
       - declare the simulator normative only for edge-aligned inputs and require
         harnesses to hold inputs across the boundary. Changes no hardware and no
         language surface, but weakens the central check: sim ≡ SV would no longer
         hold under the testbench convention every existing test uses.
     WHAT IT DOES *NOT* UNBLOCK — measured 2026-08-24, correcting an earlier
     overstatement in this file ("settling it is what would put the CPU's control
     flow within reach"). The seam was the FOURTH thing standing between
     examples/cpu/rv32i_cpu.rs and transpilation, not the first:
       1. `program: In<Vec<Bits<32>>, MainClk>` — a `Vec` input port. A
          variable-length vector is not a wire bundle; this is not a missing
          feature so much as a value that is not a signal.
       2. `let flat = { let mut v = program.read(); v.resize(1024, …); v };` —
          host-side Rust running at construction.
       3. `from_contents(clk, flat.clone())` for imem AND dmem — a run-time
          computed preload, refused because there is nothing to emit.
       4. the 17 tick-then-test waits — the seam.
     Items 1-3 are one thing: the program image arrives as a host-side Rust value,
     which is how the example is WRITTEN (to be simulated with a program handed in
     by the testbench), not a transpiler gap. A synthesizable CPU takes its program
     from a fixed image (`from_fn`, or `from_contents` with literals — both work
     now) or fetches it over a bus port.
     ALSO WORTH KNOWING before assuming the CPU's waits could just be flipped to
     the supported ordering: of the 17, EIGHT wait on a *write* port's
     `is_ready()`, which the simulator always returns true for — they are "tick
     exactly once" written in a general form, and
     `loop { if wp.is_ready() { break; } tick; }` would never tick at all, which
     the reachability guarantee rejects. Only the nine read-port waits (READ_LAT=2)
     are genuine variable waits. So a transpilable CPU is a REWRITE, not a flip.
     Pinned by unsupported_constructs.rs::ticking_before_the_test_in_a_wait_is_unsupported.

  ☐ `RegOut` IS NOT IMMUNE TO SEQUENTIAL FORWARDING, contrary to
     PRETICK_ALIGNMENT_GUARDRAIL.md, which lists it as the first remedy and calls
     it "immune: it commits at the edge, so *when* the write executes is
     unobservable". Measured: with a `RegOut` written AFTER a register update in
     the same segment, codegen emits `out <= n` — the register's OLD value — while
     the simulator forwards the update and writes the new one. The D1 compile error
     exempts `RegOut`, so this shape passes the guardrail and diverges anyway.
     The immunity claim holds for the PHASE question it was written about (when the
     segment runs); it does not extend to forwarding WITHIN the segment. Worth
     correcting in the doc either way, and deciding whether D1's exemption should
     be narrowed.

  ☐ `continue` inside a nested loop. Jumping to the loop head mid-cycle needs the
     head's LOWERED body at a point where it is still being lowered. `break` avoids
     this because its target (the loop's continuation) is plain source. Refused with
     a spanned diagnostic; pinned.

  ── LATENT HOLES SURFACED BY THE MEMORY WORK, 2026-08-24 ────────────────────
  Each of these was invisible until a memory design happened to walk into it.
  None was caught by a failing example; two were caught by a corpus guard and one
  by Verilator's linter. Recorded here because the CLASS matters — a check that
  cannot see a construct reports green on it.

  ✔ FIXED — copper-analysis counted a MEMORY ARRAY as a flip-flop.
     `reference_sv_registers` strips bracket groups before scanning for `<=`
     targets, so a memory write `mem[a] <= d` reduced to `mem <= d` and was
     reported as a register named `mem`. No source-level inference can ever produce
     that name (`infer_registers` names locals live across a tick; a `Memory<..>`
     binding is not one), so `register_reconciliation` failed the moment a fixture
     contained a memory write.
     THE HOLE WAS ALREADY THERE, unrelated to codegen: the same miscount applies to
     any HAND-WRITTEN reference SV containing a RAM, which is the G2 path —
     examples/memory/sv/dual_port_ram.sv declares `reg [15:0] ram [1023:0]` and
     writes `ram[addra] <= dia`. Anyone calling `with_reference_registers` on a
     memory design would have compared against a register set with a phantom entry.
     FIX: unpacked-array declarations are excluded in `reference_sv_registers`, with
     unit tests BOTH ways — a memory is not a register, and a bit-assigned register
     (`q[3] <= …`) still is one.
     Related, same file, same shape of blind spot: the memory read PIPELINE
     registers (`<mem>_rd<N>_q` / `_v`) are synthesized by lowering and have no
     source-level name, so register_reconciliation now treats them like the
     phase/pc counter it already excluded.

  ☐ Multi-tick loops SILENTLY DROP combinational statements after the LAST tick.
     `shir_lower::lower_seq_body`'s multi-tick path never passes the trailing
     segment through `lower_pre_edge_stmts` — only the single-tick path does (where
     the trailing segment shares the one phase). Its REGISTER updates do flow, so
     the loss is partial and quiet: an `out.write(x)` after the last tick simply
     vanished, leaving an undriven port. Measured on a two-tick module with two
     outputs: the second had no driver at all in the emitted SV.
     NOW LOUD, not fixed — it is a clean error, pinned by
     unsupported_constructs.rs::trailing_combinational_statements_in_a_multi_tick_loop_are_rejected.
     DECIDING where those statements belong is a real semantics question (the
     trailing segment runs in the NEXT iteration's phase-0 window, but the existing
     model attributes its register updates to the LAST phase's edge), so it needs
     sign-off rather than a default. Note the two halves currently disagree, which
     is worth resolving at the same time.

  ☐ Copper does not reject an UNDRIVEN `Out` — only Verilator does.
     examples/memory/dual_port_ram.rs shipped a `data_out_a` port it never wrote.
     Harmless in simulation (the wire just keeps its initial value) and invisible
     until the module transpiled, where it is a fatal UNDRIVEN warning under -Wall.
     The port was removed, but the language gap stands: an output a module never
     drives is a design error the front end could catch with the analysis it
     already has (`ports_driven_all_paths` and friends live in vlir_lower).
     Care needed on the sequential side: a plain `Out` HOLDS when unwritten (the
     enabled-register idiom, verified against BaseJump's bsg_dff_en), so the rule
     is "never written on ANY path", not "not written on all paths".


  ── TWO BUGS THE CAUSE-K GATE HAD BEEN MASKING (found 2026-08-24) ──────────

  ✔ FIXED — REACHABILITY ACCEPTED A PROGRAM THE SIMULATOR LIVELOCKS ON.
    `Cfg::build` folded a tick-bearing nested loop into ONE node with a **Tick**
    out-edge, unconditionally. The comment justified the optimism as
    "rejection-sound: a possible 0-iteration exit must not make the outer loop
    look tickless", citing uart_tx / rv32i_cpu. That is right for a counted `for`
    — it runs its body, so it ticks — and WRONG for a `loop`/`while` that can
    break before its first tick, where the zero-tick exit is a real path.
    An outer loop whose ONLY boundary is such a nested loop therefore returns to
    its top in zero time. MEASURED, not argued: with the cause-K gate relaxed,
        loop { loop { if a { break; } loop { if b { break; } tick; } } }
    transpiled to an FSM running one cycle per iteration while the SIMULATOR SPUN
    FOREVER — 99.5% CPU, 5m28s, no progress, with a low and b high. A false PASS
    of the worst kind: the transpiler inventing a clock edge the source has no
    right to.
    FIX: `Node::may_exit_without_tick`, set when folding a nested `loop`/`while`
    whose body can break before its first tick, and read ONLY by
    `check_reachability` — which now walks that node's Tick edge as a zero-time
    edge. The edge STAYS `Tick`, because liveness needs it: the loop does cross a
    clock edge on its other paths and values live across it are still registers.
    Conflating the two would have silently changed register inference.
    A nested loop that ALWAYS ticks stays a boundary (`ticks_before_any_break`,
    mutually recursive with `may_exit_without_tick`), which is what keeps
    `loop { <work>; for _ in 0..N { tick } }` — the shape cause K exists for —
    well-formed. Corpus impact measured: zero, nothing newly rejected.
    KNOWN GAP, recorded rather than relied on: a `for` over an EMPTY constant
    range would still read as a boundary. Seeing that needs const evaluation, and
    copper-analysis is syntax-level (`syn`-only) by design.
    Pinned by 5 tests in `copper-analysis/src/cfg.rs` and one in
    `copper-codegen/tests/unsupported_constructs.rs`.

  L ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24) — **A `RegOut` WRITE OF A REGISTER ALREADY ASSIGNED IN
    THE SAME SEGMENT EMITTED THE PRE-EDGE VALUE.** Pre-existing, not a
    control-flow bug, and it reproduced with no nested loop at all — found while
    building cause K's DUT.
        loop { n = n + step.read(); acc.write(n); clk.tick().await; }
    emitted `acc <= n; n <= (n + step);` — so `acc` lagged `n` by a cycle, and the
    two ORDERINGS of the same three statements emitted the SAME module. Measured
    sim ≢ SV: the simulator gave 4, 6, 14, 17 where Verilator gave 0, 4, 6, 14.
    THE RULE IS THE EMISSION CONTEXT, NOT THE SOURCE ORDER — this took two wrong
    attempts to find, both caught by existing oracles rather than by argument:
      - forwarding every read on the pre-edge path broke `branch_local_counter`;
      - keying on pre-tick vs post-tick SEGMENT broke `lfsr`.
    Both are plain-`Out` modules, and that is the point:
      * a `RegOut` drive becomes `acc <= <expr>` inside `always_ff`, evaluated with
        PRE-edge register values → it must be forwarded;
      * a plain `Out` drive becomes a continuous `assign o = <expr>`, observed
        AFTER the edge when the flop already holds the assigned value → forwarding
        it applies the update twice.
    FIX: `shir_lower::Forwarding`, keyed on `CHIRPort::registered`. Advancing the
    map is DELEGATED to `extract_updates_from_stmts` — the same walk that computes
    the registers' own next-state expressions — rather than reimplemented, so the
    `if`/`match` branch-merge rule has exactly one definition. It gets the mod-N
    counter idiom right for free:
        acc <= (((t + 8'd1) == 8'd5) ? 8'd0 : (t + 8'd1))
    Pinned by `tests/regout_forwarding_equivalence.rs`, whose sharpest assertion is
    that the two orderings do NOT emit the same module — that identity WAS the bug.
    WHY THE CORPUS NEVER CAUGHT IT: every existing module writes its `RegOut`
    BEFORE mutating (the write-before-tick Moore idiom `waiter`, `handshake` and
    the BaseJump modules all use), which is the one ordering the bug did not touch.

  L-1 ✔ FIXED (2026-08-25) — **A CONDITIONALLY-DRIVEN PLAIN `Out` HAD THE SAME
    LAG.** `vlir_lower` turns a conditionally-driven `Out` into an implicit-hold
    register in `always_ff` (`conditional_output_ports`), so it is sampled at the
    edge exactly like a `RegOut` despite its port type. Repro:
        loop { n = n + step.read(); if enable { o.write(n); } tick; }
    emitted `if (enable) o <= n;` where it must be `o <= (n + step)`. MEASURED with
    `enable` alternating: simulator 3, 3, 11, 11, 18, 18 vs Verilator 0, 0, 4, 4,
    13, 13.

  L-2 ✔ FIXED (2026-08-25) — **A `let` WIRE READ AFTER A REGISTER ASSIGN AND FED
    TO A `RegOut` LAGGED TOO.** The wire lowers to a continuous `assign` over the
    flop, so a drive sampling it at the edge missed the assignment entirely:
        loop { n = n + step.read(); let t = n + 1; acc.write(t); tick; }
    emitted `t = (n + 8'd1); acc <= t;` — the `+ step` never reached `acc`.
    MEASURED: simulator 4, 5, 12, 14, 19, 23 vs Verilator 1, 4, 5, 12, 14, 19.
    `extract_updates_from_stmts` now records `let` wires in the forwarding map, so
    the same fix covers a REGISTER whose next value reads such a wire.

  ── CAUSE N: A BRANCH DID NOT READ ITS OWN SEGMENT'S UPDATE (2026-08-25) ──────
  ✔ FIXED. `shir_lower::lower_stmt_list` runs every `PortWrite` through
  `Forwarding::at_edge` — that is the cause-L fix, and it is what makes
  `n = n + step; acc.write(n)` mean what it says. An `if` CONDITION and a `match`
  SCRUTINEE did not get the same treatment, despite a comment three lines above
  the code claiming they did. So a segment that updates a register and then
  branches on it emitted the pre-edge value:

      c = c + 1;                     if ((c == 8'd3)) begin      // pre-edge c
      if c == 3 { o.write(n); }  →       o <= n;
                                     end
                                     c <= (((c + 8'd1) == 8'd3) ? …)  // forwarded

  Both copies of the same test, one forwarded and one not — which is exactly the
  evidence the reverted counted-delay attempt recorded and misdiagnosed (see
  COUNTED DELAYS above; the correction is written up there).
  SILENT, AND WORSE THAN A CYCLE LATE. The simulator runs the segment with Rust's
  own sequencing, so the guard fires on the INCREMENTED `c`. The transpiled module
  fired a cycle late — and with `c` also reset inside the branch, i.e. the mod-N
  counter idiom, the pre-edge `c` never reaches 3 at all, the arm never fires and
  the output is DEAD. The emitted module is perfectly well-typed.
  WHY THE CORPUS WAS GREEN: `two_domain_counter` tests `count` BEFORE its update.
  Nothing in examples/ reads a register after assigning it in the same segment.
  THE FIX FOLLOWS L-1 AND FOR ITS REASON — whether a drive is edge-sampled is
  decided a stage later, by `vlir_lower`, so predicting it in SHIR would be a
  second implementation to keep in step. `SHIRStmt::If`/`Match` carry BOTH forms
  through `VLIRStmt`, and `split_output_reg` — which already splits a branch into
  an `always_comb` copy and an `always_ff` copy — takes the matching one for each.
  The `always_comb` copy keeps the UNFORWARDED test on purpose: a continuous
  assign is read after the edge, when the registers already hold their new values.
  `pattern_detector`'s Moore output depends on that and must not move.
  Both forms count as reads in `collect_vlir_reads_stmt` and `collect_read_uses`,
  so `drop_unread_wires` cannot delete a wire only the surviving form uses — the
  dead-wire consequence L-2 recorded.
  → tests/branch_on_updated_reg_equivalence.rs (the `if` half) and
    tests/match_hold_default_arm_equivalence.rs (the `match` half). Both fail
    against Verilator with the fix reverted.
  RECORD THE CLASS: a doc comment asserted the invariant, the code did not
  implement it, and no test asked. The comment made the hole *look* closed to
  every reader since — including the one who wrote the counted-delay diagnosis.

  ── A WILDCARD ARM THAT DRIVES NOTHING LOST ITS HOLD (2026-08-25) ─────────────
  ✔ FIXED, found by the `match` half of cause N's test rather than looked for.
  `vlir_lower::hoist_moore_output_defaults` gives a Moore output decoded from a
  state `case` a default at the top of the block, so an encoding the state register
  can never reach does not make the output look conditional and get wrongly
  registered. It decided "driven on every path" by intersecting the EXPLICIT arms
  only — and a `default` written in the source is the one fall-through that is
  actually reachable.
  So `match s { One => o.write(n), _ => {} }` — the enabled-register idiom, whose
  whole point is that `o` HOLDS on the cycles the arm does not fire (see "Out holds
  when unwritten", verified against bsg_dff_en) — got an unconditional `o <= n`
  hoisted over it and was driven every cycle. Measured sim ≢ Verilator: the
  simulator wrote on the even cycles, the transpiled module on all of them.
  The distinction the pass needs is between two kinds of fall-through:
  `default: None` is the case it EXISTS for (no source wildcard, only unreachable
  encodings); `default: Some(_)` is a path the author wrote, and the port is only
  fully driven if that path drives it too. An empty `_ => {}` intersects to
  nothing. `traffic_light` and `bsg_dff_en` are untouched — the narrowness the
  pass documents is preserved.
  RECORD THE CLASS, again: an "all paths" check that enumerates only the paths it
  finds convenient. Same shape as `last_update_for` keeping the FIRST of several
  updates — a set operation that quietly omits one member is right until something
  produces that member.

  ── HOW L-1 AND L-2 WERE FIXED, AND WHY THE FIRST ATTEMPT COULD NOT WORK ──────
    The cause-L fix keyed forwarding on `CHIRPort::registered` in `shir_lower`.
    That is not sound, and L-1 is the counterexample: whether a drive is sampled at
    the edge is decided a STAGE LATER, by `vlir_lower`, from
      registered `RegOut` ∪ phase-scoped `Out` ∪ conditionally-driven `Out`
    and an output that looks conditional can be un-registered again by
    `hoist_moore_output_defaults`. It is not a function of the SHIR, so predicting
    it upstream would be a second implementation to keep in step with the real one
    — the failure mode that produced cause L in the first place.
    SO THE DECISION WAS NOT MOVED, THE DATA WAS. `SHIRStmt::PortDrive` now carries
    BOTH forms — `value` (read after the edge, unforwarded) and `edge_value`
    (sampled before it, forwarded) — through `VLIRStmt::PortAssign`, and
    `split_output_reg` picks. That is the one point where a drive actually becomes
    a non-blocking assignment, so no stage has to predict another's decision.
    THE DEAD-WIRE CONSEQUENCE, worth knowing about: inlining a `let` wire into the
    edge form can leave the wire with no readers, and the equivalence harness runs
    Verilator under `-Wall`, where `UNUSEDSIGNAL` FAILS THE BUILD. So a correct
    lowering was being rejected for a lint. `vlir_lower::drop_unread_wires` removes
    them, iterating to a fixpoint. It lives in VLIR and not SHIR for the same
    reason as the choice itself: liveness is only exact once each drive has
    committed to one form — a wire read solely by the discarded form still looks
    live at SHIR level, and the pass was tried there first and did not fire. Its
    collectors match every enum EXHAUSTIVELY (no `_` arm) so that a variant added
    later fails to compile rather than silently causing a live wire to be deleted.
    Pinned by `tests/regout_forwarding_equivalence.rs` (6 tests), including a shape
    pin that the inlined wire is gone.

